  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nThis afternoon...Temperatures are near or a few degrees higher\nthan at this time yesterday. Today will likely be warmer than\nyesterday, with high temperatures anywhere from a few degrees\nabove normal in the deserts to as much as 10-15 degrees above\nnormal in the inland valleys of San Diego County. There were \npatchy marine layer low clouds this morning which have cleared\nout. Southwest flow aloft ahead of a low pressure system to the\nnorthwest has brought a few high clouds today.\n\nSunday and Monday...The low to the northwest will move southeastward\ninto SoCal by Monday while hurricane Odalys (currently about 950 \nmiles southwest of San Diego) slowly moves north and weakens, \nbecoming a remnant low about 500 miles southwest of San Diego by \nMonday. The flow aloft will draw mid and high level moisture from \nOdalys into SoCal, resulting in slight chances for mostly light \nprecipitation Sunday and Monday. The best chances for rainfall \naccumulations will be in the mountains during the afternoons. \nCurrent estimates are for rainfall totals range from 0.00 in \nOrange County up to 0.15 inch in the mountains. This scenario will\nbring lower temperatures, and Monday is expected to be the \ncoolest day with temperatures near normal in the coastal areas to \nas much as 10-15 degrees below normal in the mountains. The \nincreasing mid and high clouds could disrupt the marine layer and \nhinder the formation of low clouds tonight through Monday night.\nAs the low pressure moves in from the northwest, it will bring\nstronger onshore flow on Monday, with westerly winds gusting \n30-40 mph in the wind-prone locations of the mountains and \nadjacent desert slopes.\n\nFor Tuesday through the end of next week...The low pressure system\nbegins to move eastward into AZ on Tuesday and high pressure aloft\nmoves into SoCal. This will bring dry and warmer weather with the\npotential for weak offshore flow on Tuesday and Wednesday. The\nwarming begins on Tuesday and peaks next Saturday with daytime\nhigh temperatures up to 7 degrees above normal in the deserts and\nas much as 10-15 degrees above normal in the low elevations west\nof the mountains. The lack of mid and high clouds with high \npressure aloft could allow the low clouds of a shallow marine\nlayer to return to the coastal areas.",
